rough the block's j values, so repeated loads. Better to parallelize over j-block? Hmm.\n\nAlternative: tile both i and j with sequential accumulation per i across tiles. For each i-tile (set of i), loop over all j sequentially; that's previous tiling. That gives reuse of p[j] across multiple i in the tile, reducing memory traffic by factor tile size. With SoA, same benefit.\n\nSo we can implement tiling: choose block size maybe 64 or based on cache. For each ib_start from 0 to n step IB:\n  #pragma omp parallel for\n  for (int ii=ib_start; ii<min(ib_start+IB,n); ++ii) {\n    compute forces using all j sequentially.\n  }\nThis preserves order per i because j loop full sequential inside. OpenMP over ii within tile gives parallelism, and each thread reads x[j] etc across all j; but multiple threads in same tile read same j data concurrently, causing redundant memory traffic? Actually if IB is small (e.g., number of cores), then only 4 i's processed at a time, so p[j] loaded once per cache line for those 4 i's? With SoA and sequential access across j, each thread reads x[0..n-1]; four threads read same data concurrently but from L3 maybe; still memory bandwidth may be bottleneck. Better to parallelize over tiles (outer) rather than within tile: process different i-tiles in parallel, each thread handles a contiguous block of i's and loops all j for its own i's sequentially. That gives reuse across i within a thread's tile, and no redundant reads across threads because each thread has distinct i range but still reads same x[j] arrays; however if tiles are disjoint i ranges, each thread still reads entire x array per i, so total memory traffic is n^2 regardless (each i reads all j). Tiling doesn't reduce O(n^2) traffic unless you also block j and reuse across multiple i simultaneously. To reduce traffic, need to process a tile of i's together for a given j-block: load that j-block into cache once and use it for all i in the tile. That requires accumulating partial forces per i across j-blocks.\n\nLet's design blocked algorithm preserving exact order:\n- Allocate force arrays fx[n], fy[n], fz[n] initialized to 0.\n- Choose block size JB (e.g., 256).\n- For each j_block_start = 0; j_block_start < n; j_block_start += JB:\n    int j_end = min(j_block_start+JB, n);\n    // Process all i in parallel? But for each i, loop over j from j_block_start to j_end and accumulate into fx[i].\n    #pragma omp parallel for\n    for (int i=0; i<n; ++i) {\n       float xi=x[i], yi=y[i], zi=z[i];\n       float fxi=fx[i], fyi=fy[i], fzi=fz[i]; // local accumulators? But we need to add contributions from this block. We can compute partial sums locally and then update fx[i] after loop.\n       for (int j=j_block_start; j<j_end; ++j) { ... }\n       fx[i] += fxi_partial; ...\n    }\nThis preserves order because outer loop over j blocks is sequential, inner i parallel. For each i, contributions from block 0 added first, then block 1, etc., so total sum order is same as original (all j in increasing order). Within a block, j sequential. This is correct.\n\nMemory locality: for each j-block, we load x[j],y[j],z[j] for that block once and reuse across all i? Actually inner loop over j reads those arrays; but outer parallel over i means each thread loops through same j_block range, so the data may be loaded multiple times by different threads. However if JB is small enough to fit in L1/L2, after first thread loads it, others can hit cache. With 4 cores sharing L3 maybe okay. But we could also parallelize over i-tiles within each j-block? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, use OpenMP collapse(2) on both loops? That would reorder summation per i if j loop split across threads; not safe.\n\nGiven previous tiling achieved 0.223s, perhaps simple SoA + OpenMP over i might be similar or better. Let's estimate: baseline sequential 0.67s; with 4 cores ideal speedup ~4 -> 0.168s plus overhead. Previous got 0.223 (3x). Maybe memory bandwidth limits. SoA could improve cache efficiency and vectorization? But compiler may auto-vectorize inner loop if using -O3 and restrict, but floating-point reassociation not allowed; however it can still vectorize with horizontal sum? Actually to compute fx += dx*inv_dist3 for each j, that's a reduction across j. Without fast-math, cannot vectorize because order matters. So no SIMD.\n\nSo main gain is parallelization and cache blocking. Our previous tiling likely used OpenMP over tiles; we can replicate but with SoA maybe slightly better due to contiguous arrays reducing TLB misses? Let's propose a candidate that combines:\n- SoA layout.\n- Blocked j-loop with sequential accumulation per i across blocks, using force accumulators array and OpenMP parallelization over i within each block. This gives both parallelism and cache reuse of j-block data.\n\nBut we need to ensure exact output bytes: The order of additions for each body is exactly original because outer loop over j-blocks in increasing order; inner j sequential; accumulation into fx[i] after each block adds partial sums sequentially. However note that within a block, we compute local sum and then add to existing fx[i]. This changes the grouping: originally all terms added one by one into single accumulator; now we have multiple accumulators (one per block) combined at end. Floating-point addition is not associative, so results may differ! For example original: ((a+b)+c)+d... Blocked: first compute s1 = a+b+c (for block), then fx += s1; next block s2=d+e..., fx += s2. This changes rounding because intermediate sums are rounded differently. So exact bytes would likely fail unless tolerance? But previous tiling preserved order exactly by not splitting j loop for each i; it processed all j sequentially per i, just grouped multiple i's together. That preserves arithmetic exactly. Our blocked j approach does NOT preserve exact summation order. Therefore cannot use if strict equality.\n\nSo we must keep inner j loop sequential and unbroken for each i to match bas