\nvoid body_force(const float *restrict x,\n                const float *restrict y,\n                const float *restrict z,\n                float *restrict vx,\n                float *restrict vy,\n                float *restrict vz,\n                float dt, int n) {\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    float xi = x[i];\n    float yi = y[i];\n    float zi = z[i];\n    float fx = 0.0f;\n    float fy = 0.0f;\n    float fz = 0.0f;\n\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n      float dx = x[j] - xi;\n      float dy = y[j] - yi;\n      float dz = z[j] - zi;\n      float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n      float inv_dist = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n      float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n\n      fx += dx * inv_dist3;\n      fy += dy * inv_dist3;\n      fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n    }\n\n    vx[i] += dt*fx;\n    vy[i] += dt*fy;\n    vz[i] += dt*fz;\n  }\n}\n\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) { ... same ... }\n\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) { ... same ... }\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n  int nbodies;\n  Body *bodies = read_dataset(&nbodies);\n\n  float *x = malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  float *y = malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  float *z = malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  float *vx = malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  float *vy = malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  float *vz = malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n\n  for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n    x[i] = bodies[i].x;\n    y[i] = bodies[i].y;\n    z[i] = bodies[i].z;\n    vx[i] = bodies[i].vx;\n    vy[i] = bodies[i].vy;\n    vz[i] = bodies[i].vz;\n  }\n\n  for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; ++iter) {\n    body_force(x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, DT, nbodies);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n      x[i] += vx[i] * DT;\n      y[i] += vy[i] * DT;\n      z[i] += vz[i] * DT;\n    }\n  }\n\n  for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n    bodies[i].x = x[i];\n    bodies[i].y = y[i];\n    bodies[i].z = z[i];\n    bodies[i].vx = vx[i];\n    bodies[i].vy = vy[i];\n    bodies[i].vz = vz[i];\n  }\n\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\n\n  free(x); free(y); free(z); free(vx); free(vy); free(vz);\n  free(bodies);\n\n  exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n}\n```\n\nNeed to include <omp.h>? Not necessary for pragmas; but if we want to ensure OpenMP enabled at compile time, the Makefile likely has -fopenmp. We don't control Makefile (only src files). The problem statement says can modify source code only under src/. So we cannot change Makefile. But previous experiment used OpenMP and passed, so compiler flags include -fopenmp presumably from default? Actually baseline may not have OpenMP; but our previous experiment with tiling+OpenMP succeeded, meaning the build system likely compiles with -O3 -fopenmp or at least supports pragmas (ignored if no flag). If -fopenmp absent, pragma ignored and code runs sequential, still correct but slower. We need to ensure correctness regardless; OpenMP pragmas are safe.\n\nHowever we must be careful: `#pragma omp parallel for` without including <omp.h> is fine; compiler recognizes if -fopenmp set. If not, it's just a comment? Actually unknown pragma ignored with warning maybe. So okay.\n\nPotential issue: The conversion loops and final copy add overhead but negligible.\n\nWe should also consider using `posix_memalign` for alignment to help vectorization of position update loop; but not necessary.\n\nLet's think if there is any way to improve further within role A without breaking exact arithmetic. Maybe use cache blocking on i with OpenMP over tiles as previous, but SoA may make it faster due to better prefetching. We could combine both: tile i-loop and parallelize over tiles, while using SoA. That might be even better because each thread processes a contiguous range of i's, reading x,y,z arrays sequentially for each i; with SoA, the inner j loop streams through three arrays, good cache behavior. Tiling doesn't change arithmetic per i (full j loop). We can choose tile size maybe 64 or based on n/4? But previous tiling likely used something like block=... We don't know.\n\nLet's propose a candidate that uses SoA and OpenMP over i directly; simpler and likely competitive. The role is memory locality/data layout, so this fits.\n\nWe need to include transformations list: \"Convert Body array-of-structs to separate structure-of-arrays for positions/velocities\", \"Parallelize force computation across bodies with OpenMP while preserving per-body summation order\", \"Hoist body i coordinates out of inner loop and add restrict qualifiers\".\n\nHypothesis: SoA layout improves cache line utilization during the O(n^2) pair interactions because each coordinate stream is contiguous, reducing memory traffic; combined with OpenMP over independent bodies yields near-linear speedup on 4 cores while preserving exact floating-point summation order per body.\n\nSources_kept must be [] since integration_source_ids empty.\n\nNotes: mention that we preserve arithmetic by keeping full j loop sequential per i and only parallelizing across i.\n\nLet's ensure code compiles with C99? Use `restrict` keyword; in C, restrict is standard. Need to include <stdlib.h> for malloc/free already there.\n\nOne subtlety: The original body_force used float dt parameter but didn't use it except update after loop; we keep same.\n\nAlso note that the baseline's inner loop uses p[j].x - p[i].x etc; our code uses x[j] - xi, identical values. So arithmetic exactly same order and operations? Yes: dx = x[j]-xi vs p[j].x-p[i].x; same float subtraction. sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING; same expression evaluation order (left-to-right for +). inv_dist = 1/sqrt(sqrd_dist); we use 1.0f / sqrt(...) vs original `1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist)` where 1 is int, converted to float? In C, `1 / sqrt(...)` performs integer division? Actually `/` with one operand int and other double/float: if sqrt returns double (since math.h sqrt takes double), then 1 is converted to double, res